# Human review and acceptance

Created: 2026-11-04T21:45:23Z (first recorded addition in Git)
Last updated: 2026-11-10T15:55:25Z

## Purpose and applicability

The human is an active implementation partner and must be able to explain
the work in their own words, including its role in the project and relevant
limitations.

This rule complements [coauthoring_protocol.mdc](coauthoring_protocol.mdc).
Coursework follows its applicable course instructions.

## Understanding over memorization

Consult code, documentation, and recorded reasoning as needed.
Review is not a closed-book test or a requirement to memorize every detail.

Use those references to develop and check understanding rather than merely
repeat an agent-generated explanation.

## Proportionate review

Review the actual changes and relevant verification before accepting substantive
work, including agent-authored changes.

Trace important behavior through the implementation where needed. Ask questions
and investigate unfamiliar decisions, unexplained behavior, or uncertain results.

Scale review to the change. Small, obvious edits can use a quick inspection;
they do not require a written questionnaire.

## Outcomes and gaps

Record human acceptance or unresolved concerns in the existing task record,
following [documentation_core.mdc](documentation_core.mdc).

Make gaps visible and agree on the next action. If reasoning is missing, use
[documentation_debrief.mdc](documentation_debrief.mdc) to preserve it in the
appropriate location.

The agent may explain, ask questions, challenge assumptions, and identify gaps.
It must not certify human understanding or record acceptance without human
confirmation.

Follow the checkpoint status distinctions in
[coauthoring_protocol.mdc](coauthoring_protocol.mdc).
Human acceptance does not itself authorize additional implementation, commits,
or merges.
